1. Direct typing instead of vars for C# 
2. No sinlge line if
3. No or very minimal (critical casese) inline CSS
4. Minimal logs for only critical cases, self described code and namings
5. Minimal comments for critical cases only, for other places rely on code readability; no section-label comments (// state, // setup, etc.)
6. Try keep cyclomatic complexity of methods and functions under 10
7. JS/TS class member order (shared, react, vue): public/private fields → constructor → public methods → private methods (#prefix)
8. One blank line between class members
9. Don't expose members that could be private; minimize public surface area

## CSS / BEM

10. Use BEM naming: `.block`, `.block__element`, `.block--modifier`, `.block__element--modifier`
11. One CSS file per block (e.g. button.css, card.css, modal.css). Exceptions: variables.css, global.css, utilities.css
12. Compound element/modifier names use hyphens: `.block__header-cell`, `.block--secondary-active` (valid)
13. Never attach an element to a modifier: `.block--modifier__element` is forbidden. Elements always belong to the block. Use a scoped selector instead: `.block--modifier .block__element { }`
14. Never chain multiple modifiers with `--`: `.block--mod1--mod2` is forbidden. Use `.block--mod1-mod2`
